    "body": "The gold price has hit its lowest point since September 2025. Credit: Adobe Stock Photo by monsitj.\nPosted By: Colin McClelland November 20, 2026\nBets on a December U.S. rate increase helped drive gold towards $3,500 an oz. Friday, testing miners’ budgets after all-in sustaining costs rose 22% year on year in the second quarter, the steepest rise since 2013, according to the World Gold Council.\nSpot gold traded at $3,576.25 per oz. at 9:59 a.m. Friday in New York, according to GoldPrice.com . Bullion had touched $3,513.54 earlier in the session, its lowest since September 2025. Traders put the odds of a quarter-point rate increase at 99%, according to CME FedWatch.\nHigher Treasury yields make bonds more attractive to investors who might otherwise hold gold, which pays no interest. A stronger dollar makes bullion more expensive for buyers using other currencies.\nAs technology stocks fell, “bullion has become a source of cash for investors facing mounting losses elsewhere,” Ole Hansen , head of commodity strategy at Denmark’s Saxo Bank, wrote Friday.\nMiners spent a record $441 (C$631) per oz. on capital to sustain output in the second quarter, up 34% from the first. The increase in that spending accounted for about 80% of the quarterly rise in all-in sustaining costs to $1,930 per oz., Oliver Blagden of London-based precious metals consultancy Metals Focus wrote in a Nov. 10 analysis published by the gold council.\nMargins\nThe margin above those costs fell 16% from the first quarter to $2,582 per oz., leaving less cash per ounce for new projects and shareholder returns. It was still the second-highest margin Metals Focus has recorded.\nNearly all Federal Reserve policymakers expected further rate increases to curb inflation, according to minutes released Wednesday. Meanwhile, gold holdings in exchange-traded funds tracked by Bloomberg were little changed over the past month despite a roughly 10% price drop, Saxo’s Hansen said.\nBarrick Mining (TSX: ABX; NYSE: B) realized $174 less for each ounce it sold in the third quarter while all-in sustaining costs rose $45 to $1,911 per oz. That narrowed the margin by $219 to $2,332 an ounce, its quarterly figures show.\nMiners produced 1,071 tonnes worldwide in the third quarter, up 3% year on year and a record for that quarter, the council said in its quarterly review . New mines including Goose in Nunavut helped lift Canadian output 22%.\nGoose\nB2Gold (TSX: BTO; NYSE-A: BTG) sold 17,426 oz. of gold from its Goose mine in Nunavut in the second quarter , when an April fire in the crushing circuit reduced the amount of ore the mill could process. The mine sold fewer ounces than expected and incurred costs it had budgeted for the first quarter, pushing all-in sustaining costs to $6,390 per oz. sold, B2Gold said.\nGoose sold 30,894 oz. in the third quarter , spreading costs over more ounces. All-in sustaining costs at the mine fell 25% to $4,816 per oz. sold, while B2Gold’s companywide average rose to $2,602 from $2,356.\nThe VanEck Gold Miners ETF (NYSE Arca: GDX) closed Thursday at $72 in New York, 12% weaker than the previous Friday.\nAgnico\nOn Wednesday, Ralph Profiti, a mining analyst at U.S. investment bank Stifel, asked Agnico Eagle Mines (TSX, NYSE: AEM) whether $3,500 gold would force it to postpone projects or cut exploration.\n“So, really nothing changes at $3,500 gold. I’d suggest nothing changes at $3,000 gold,” Agnico chief financial officer Jamie Porter said at the briefing in Toronto.\nAgnico’s sustaining spending rose from $237 million in the second quarter to $287 million in the third, including $96 million at Detour Lake in Ontario, the company reported in October.\nCanada’s largest gold producer by market cap and output expects capital spending to exceed $3.5 billion next year, including growth projects and capitalized exploration, Porter said. Its main projects remain on track, though higher drilling rates could put off some exploration.\nPrint\nCOMMODITY: Gold\nREGION: Canada United States\nCOMPANY: Agnico Eagle Mines B2Gold Barrick Mining\nRepublish this article",
